rules:
- compliance:
    enabled: true
  id: rule-adr-single-source-of-truth
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Architecture decisions must be recorded and updated only in decision records
    (ADR); all other representations (pattern metadata, AEM, decision workflows) must
    be derived from ADR to prevent inconsistencies.
  title: Rule Adr Single Source Of Truth
- id: rule-aem-consistency-with-decisions
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: AEM must remain consistent with ADR decisions and must never contradict
    them. AEM may include vision, roadmap, and evolution items, but these must not
    contradict existing decisions.
  title: Rule Aem Consistency With Decisions
- id: rule-architecture-work-authority
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Architecture Work is the authoritative source of architectural planning
    state; generated artifacts such as AEM, AID, and alignment engine outputs must
    derive planning status from Architecture Work rather than maintain independent
    planning state.
  title: Rule Architecture Work Authority
- id: rule-core-patterns-must-be-foundational
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Core patterns must represent foundational architectural primitives, not
    specific strategies or implementations, to maintain long-term stability.
  title: Rule Core Patterns Must Be Foundational
- compliance:
    enabled: true
  id: rule-decision-schema-must-be-structured
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: All decisions must be expressed using a structured YAML decision schema,
    not unstructured narrative text, to enable machine processing, UI rendering, and
    consistent interpretation. The ADR schema must separate architect decision from
    governance approval.
  title: Rule Decision Schema Must Be Structured
- id: rule-decision-status-drives-stage
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: 'Lifecycle stage of architecture items must be derived from decision status:
    proposed maps to NEXT/REVIEW, accepted may map to DONE, rejected must not appear
    active, deferred maps to FUTURE/DEFER; items with decision.status != ''accepted''
    must not be marked DONE.'
  title: Rule Decision Status Drives Stage
- id: rule-decisions-must-be-persisted
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: All architectural decisions must be persisted as structured ADRs before
    being reflected in AEM or other artefacts, ensuring traceability and a single
    source of truth.
  title: Rule Decisions Must Be Persisted
- id: rule-do-not-merge-across-architectural-layers
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Patterns must not be merged or consolidated across different architectural
    layers; separation between governance, execution, contracts, and reliability concerns
    must be preserved.
  title: Rule Do Not Merge Across Architectural Layers
- id: rule-generated-artifacts-not-authoritative
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Generated governance artifacts such as AEM, AID, alignment reports, and
    dashboards must summarize authoritative sources and must not become the authoritative
    source themselves.
  title: Rule Generated Artifacts Not Authoritative
- id: rule-heuristic-patterns-require-signals
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: All heuristic-derived patterns must include at least one linked signal
    as evidence, ensuring traceability and grounding in observed behaviour or insights.
  title: Rule Heuristic Patterns Require Signals
- id: rule-language-semantic-contract
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: 'Foundational global rule defining the semantic contract for architecture
    artefacts: intent artefacts must align with system state, decisions are not system
    state until governance-approved, patterns must not be described as system state
    unless approved, lifecycle terminology must be precise, and terminology must remain
    consistent across AEM, AID, ADR, and alignment artefacts.'
  title: Rule Language Semantic Contract
- compliance:
    enabled: true
  id: rule-pattern-decision-lifecycle-alignment
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: 'Pattern maturity and decision lifecycle are distinct but must be interpreted
    consistently: exploring/proposed is validating (NEXT), stable/accepted is active
    (DONE), rejected is discarded; maturity must not be conflated with governance
    approval.'
  title: Rule Pattern Decision Lifecycle Alignment
- id: rule-patterns-born-as-supporting
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: All patterns must be created as supporting by default and never directly
    as core; patterns are validated through evidence and usage before becoming foundational.
  title: Rule Patterns Born As Supporting
- id: rule-precedence-hierarchy
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: 'Defines precedence for resolving rule conflicts: Global Rules > Project
    Rules > Prompt Rules. Global rules are hard constraints that cannot be overridden;
    project rules must not violate global rules; prompt rules defer to both; conflicts
    must be detected and resolved using this hierarchy.'
  title: Rule Precedence Hierarchy
- id: rule-promotion-requires-evidence
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Pattern promotion to core must be supported by strong evidence, preferably
    from multiple sources or systems; weak or context-specific patterns must not be
    promoted.
  title: Rule Promotion Requires Evidence
- id: rule-shared-knowledge-workspace
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Projects participate in a shared Knowledge Workspace and must not create
    duplicate workspace-level governance structures when an existing workspace is
    available; onboarding may create project directories but must not recreate global,
    runtime, or templates structures that already exist.
  title: Rule Shared Knowledge Workspace
- id: rule-signal-promotion-criteria
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: 'A candidate signal may be promoted to a pattern only when it satisfies
    all mandatory criteria: recurrence, system impact, reusability, stability, and
    supporting evidence from alignment findings, architecture work, or repeated runs;
    otherwise it must remain a candidate signal.'
  title: Rule Signal Promotion Criteria
- id: rule-system-proposes-architect-decides
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: All architectural decisions must be proposed by the system and explicitly
    accepted, rejected, or deferred by an architect, maintaining human-in-the-loop
    control.
  title: Rule System Proposes Architect Decides
- id: rule-workflow-execution-model-compliance
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: 'All runtime-native workflows must conform to the CAS Workflow Execution
    Model: CAS Runner orchestrates, workflows reason, Knowledge Providers supply knowledge;
    workflows must consume supplied context, not perform repository discovery or workspace
    scanning, not assume repository structure, conform to output schemas, and follow
    onboarding lifecycle boundaries.'
  title: Rule Workflow Execution Model Compliance
summary:
  global_rules: 19
  project_rules: 0
  total_rules: 19
